Installed twelve original lessons after `@physics-statistical-review`: `physics-quantum-evidence`, `physics-quantum-state`, `physics-quantum-observable`, `physics-quantum-measurement`, `physics-quantum-mixtures`, `physics-quantum-uncertainty`, `physics-quantum-dynamics`, `physics-quantum-wavefunction`, `physics-quantum-composite`, `physics-quantum-observations`, `physics-quantum-study`, `physics-quantum-review`. Physics now has **110 common learning Records**. The new operator bridge supplies the complex algebra, projectors, metric and exponential proofs; existing waves, chemical states, probability/uncertainty and statistical counts are reused by exact references. `operator_study.py`, `periodic_study.py` and `statistical_study.py` remain unchanged. No existing shared learning Record gained another core edge.

The block separates preparation, transformation, observation and empirical evidence. Born probabilities and the ideal projective instrument are declared physical premises; algebra alone does not derive them. It develops phase/interference, finite spin observables and their units, conditional versus forgotten outcomes versus coherent recombination, density mixtures/Bloch geometry, state-dependent uncertainty, a constant Hermitian Hamiltonian and its phase differences. A normalized infinite-well reconstruction connects finite state models to spatial density and operator boundaries; an original two-mode comparison derives a left-half probability of 1/2+[4/(3π)]cos[(E₂−E₁)t/ℏ]. That continuous derivation is analytical, not part of the finite matrix program's numerical evidence.

A concrete tensor-coordinate introduction supplies the mathematics for two distinguishable two-state systems. It contrasts an entangled Bell state with a classical correlated lottery: equal z correlations do not imply equal x correlations. Joint probabilities, product factorization and local marginals are derived without claiming a signaling mechanism. A detection model then gives two different preparation/efficiency accounts with the same recorded plus/minus/lost masses; keeping loss counts alone does not identify all parameters. The count-law reuse is a combinatorial identity and does not assign thermal equilibrium to the experiment.

New source `physics-source-quantum` identifies Barton Zwiebach's MIT 8.05 Fall 2013 course and current OCW CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 terms/component exceptions. Actual note-2 passages (17 September 2013; introductory discussion and printed 5–9) were inspected, including a visual check of page 6/Figure 5. Its z-plus → retained x-minus → z sequence is explicitly a teaching/thought model: final survivor probabilities one-half each, original post-preparation input probabilities one-quarter each, half rejected. Note 6 (4 November 2013) printed 10–17 supplies the inspected dynamics discussion; note 8 (14 December 2013) printed 1–3 and 8–10 supplies finite composition and Φ₀. These are bounded model reconstructions, not raw laboratory-data analysis or reenactments.

The existing `mat-tong-quantum` source gained an inspection note only. Its author page and authorized 133-page notes were rechecked; selected printed passages 3–4, 16–17, 24–26, 76–80 and 84–86 were read. The new uncertainty lesson qualifies the page-85 noncommutation statement with a three-coordinate common-eigenvector counterexample; the correct bound depends on the state's commutator expectation. Page-78 measurement-disturbance language is also kept within preparation/instrument conditions, since an ideal eigenstate measurement can leave the state unchanged. General source rights and broader assignments remain unchanged. No external media or author code entered the repository.

